WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:06.330
Speaker 1: but hey, folks, there are a couple
of bright spots.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:12.870
And one of the bright spots that we're seeing
in the United States today is that it appears

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:20.020
that the Republican Party to a tiny, tiny
degree, appears to be warming up to the idea

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.180
of renewable energy.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:27.060
There was a great op ed in the Hill this week
where they actually talked about a piece of

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:32.520
legislation that Republicans are pushing in
North Carolina without Democratic input.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:38.000
But this piece of legislation would shut down
five major coal fired power plants in North

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.440
Carolina in the coming decade, cutting carbon
dioxide emissions and improving air quality.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.780
Importantly, the legislation mandates the
main North Carolina utility, Duke Energy,

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.069
securitize the cost of coal retirements.

00:00:52.069 --> 00:00:56.680
That provision will save ratepayers from paying
the retirement costs of those facilities and

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:59.769
is something that could be replicated in other
states across the country.

00:00:59.769 --> 00:01:03.699
To date, only a few other states have adopted
a similar policy.

00:01:03.699 --> 00:01:10.350
The bill would grow renewable energy substantially
by 4700 megawatts over the next decade, including

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:16.619
from solar power and offshore wind able to
power millions of homes.

00:01:16.619 --> 00:01:19.040
So we know North Carolina is a purple state.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.100
And you've got a Democratic governor and a
Republican controlled legislature, but this

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:29.140
bill was crafted without the input of Democrats
in the state of North Carolina.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:34.929
And it actually includes we're going to close
down some coal plants and we're going to create

00:01:34.929 --> 00:01:37.920
renewable energy.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.799
And Republicans did it on their own.

00:01:41.799 --> 00:01:51.020
As somebody who has spent 17 years covering
environmental news, reporting on it, writing

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:53.859
it, investigating it.

00:01:53.859 --> 00:02:01.080
I'm almost in shock, folks, I am almost legitimately
in shock.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:04.229
Because I never thought we'd see the day.

00:02:04.229 --> 00:02:12.510
Honestly, truly, 17 years writing about misinformation
on climate change, coming from the right,

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:20.440
writing about fossil fuel donations and how
they're paying off politicians, I'm so incredibly

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:24.870
inspired by this piece of legislation.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:27.310
And of course, we can quibble all day, does
it go far enough?

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:29.190
No, probably not.

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:30.190
But you know what, guys?

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:32.770
Sometimes you got to take a win is a win.

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:36.670
And I'm sick and tired of the all or nothing
types.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:40.500
Sometimes even a little progress is something
to be celebrated, because you know what, when

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:45.410
you celebrate the small victories and the
folks see you out there congratulating them

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:50.690
and applauding them for doing the small steps,
do you know what it encourages them, encourages

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:52.160
them to do?

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:56.880
Take another step, do it again, do more.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:58.880
And sometimes that's what we need to do.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.480
More carrot, less stick.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:02.480
Right.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:07.910
So I'm not going to criticize this piece of
legislation right now, if they can get this

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:11.850
passed and they can shut down some of those
Duke Energy coal fired power plants in the

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:17.130
state of North Carolina, then more power to
them, replace it with renewable energy.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:19.590
That's even better.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:23.620
This is a good piece of legislation.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:29.680
And I think we need to be celebrating that
fact, we get so few victories, especially

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:32.900
for the environment folks.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:39.010
So I think we need to celebrate when that
opportunity arises, when it presents itself,

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:45.040
take advantage of it, because, again, we don't
get a lot of this and hopefully other Republicans

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:47.680
around the country take note.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.790
The issue of climate change has been partizan
here in the United States for too long, but

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:58.860
ironically, it's actually not that partizan
when you go out to the voters themselves.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:04.420
Majority of Republicans support actions to
protect the environment from the effects of

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:06.550
climate change.

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:11.970
They support renewable energy, as do obviously
huge majorities of Democrats.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:15.590
And that's the thing like when you go out
there to the people, there's not this same

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:24.820
divide that you see in Washington, D.C. that
is not representative of the actual country.

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:29.910
So that's where my hope lies, my hope lies
in the people, and I think if we continue

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:34.490
to applaud these steps and more and more people
do that.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:39.470
We might just start getting some good legislation
and of course, the infrastructure bill, right,

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:44.900
that has investments in renewable, we had
some Republicans vote to support it.

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:47.500
That's a good thing.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:50.310
Renewable energy equals jobs for the United
States.

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:53.660
You know, if you want to look at it, not in
terms of environmental impact, if you want

00:04:53.660 --> 00:05:00.160
to look at it in dollars and cents, it makes
perfect sense and it makes a lot of sense

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:03.490
as well.

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:07.270
Celebrate the victories when you can, folks,
because again, too few and far between

